WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:09.568
Let's understand LoTR — Logic-of-Thought Routing — a plug-and-play way to make language models reason better, with frozen weights and no fine-tuning.

2
00:00:09.748 --> 00:00:16.708
The shift: don't just tell the model how to reason on the outside — route each step through the internal pathway its logic needs.

3
00:00:17.128 --> 00:00:29.848
Modern reasoning leans on external strategies — chain-of-thought, plan-and-solve, self-refine, self-consistency, best-of-N, constrained beam, tree search. They all prescribe how the model should reason.

4
00:00:30.268 --> 00:00:39.700
But whatever strategy you prescribe, the model runs it through the same fixed internal pathway — never checking if that pathway matches the logic the strategy induced.

5
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:46.144
So part of the benefit is lost — a strong procedure run by a static interior that never adapts.

6
00:00:46.564 --> 00:00:57.028
LoTR's observation: reasoning isn't uniform inside. Different steps put the model into different logic states — recurring internal execution regimes.

7
00:00:57.208 --> 00:01:05.392
And every state recurs across many paradigms — logic is a shared internal basis, not a by-product of one strategy.

8
00:01:05.812 --> 00:01:14.668
Each logic state couples to a distinct set of attention heads — a stable pathway template. Read the state, and you know which heads carry the next step.

9
00:01:15.088 --> 00:01:24.544
Offline, LoTR pools step readouts across paradigms into six logic states, and a light linear probe learns to read the current state at each layer.

10
00:01:24.724 --> 00:01:33.244
Then, with the model frozen, it gradient-tunes one head-gate template per state — raising the likelihood of correct steps against incorrect ones.

11
00:01:33.664 --> 00:01:43.216
Online, it's a simple plug-in. At each sentence boundary, the probe infers the state mixture and blends the cached templates into soft gates over attention heads.

12
00:01:43.396 --> 00:01:49.420
The prompt and search stay untouched — only the internal pathway adapts, step by step.

13
00:01:49.840 --> 00:02:01.120
Does it work? On eight benchmarks by eight paradigms, LoTR lifts the average by two-point-seven points — a five-point-four percent relative gain — and every benchmark improves.

14
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:07.324
It helps or ties in fifty-one of sixty-four cells — across every paradigm tested.

15
00:02:07.744 --> 00:02:18.880
And it's almost free: no extra samples or search calls — total decoding tokens shift by just zero-point-one-five percent. The gain is from routing, not a bigger budget.

16
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:30.124
It transfers, too — plus two-point-one points on a fourteen-billion model, nearly a point on a mixture-of-experts model — and beats the mean of four intervention baselines on every backbone.

17
00:02:30.544 --> 00:02:40.840
Why? Collapse the six states into one and the gain disappears — and fixed, state-blind steering baselines trail it too. It's the logic-indexed routing doing the work.

18
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:48.940
The takeaway is a change of control: don't only prescribe how to reason — match the internal pathway to the logic each step needs.

19
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:59.392
LoTR reads the state, routes the right heads, and plugs into existing reasoning paradigms — frozen, cheap, adaptive. Route the pathway to the logic.

